# Security

Applies when **the change touches auth, input parsing, secrets, or a trust
boundary**. Load for login, payment, file upload, external API calls,
migrations of credential stores. Not every chat.

## Trust boundaries

- Every input from outside the process (HTTP, CLI args, files, env, queue
  messages) is untrusted. Validate at the boundary; never carry untrusted
  data into the core.
- Validate type, length, format, and ranges. Reject early; fail closed.
- Auth checks happen at the edge, not deep in the core. The core assumes
  the caller is authorized.

## Secrets

- Never hardcode secrets. Read from env / secret manager.
- Never log secrets (tokens, passwords, API keys, PII). Redact before
  logging; assume the log is public.
- `.env` and credential files are never committed. If you see one staged,
  unstage and warn the user.

## Auth

- Hash passwords with a slow KDF (argon2, bcrypt, scrypt). Never MD5/SHA
  for passwords.
- Tokens: short-lived, scoped, revocable. Rotate via refresh, not long-lived
  access tokens.
- Constant-time comparison for tokens / hashes. No early-return on mismatch.

## Injection

- Parameterize queries. No string-built SQL / shell / LDAP.
- Escape at the output boundary (HTML, URL, JS) — not by hand-rolling.

## Do not

- Roll your own crypto. Use the stdlib / audited library.
- Trust client-side validation. The server re-validates everything.
- Ship a secret in a URL / query param (it gets logged).
